To force a text field to display columns in Adobe Flash with ActionScript, you can use the following code snippet. This example assumes you are using ActionScript 3.0:

```actionscript
import flash.text.TextField;
import flash.text.TextFormat;
import flash.text.TextFieldAutoSize;
import flash.text.TextFormatAlign;

var myTextField:TextField = new TextField();
var myTextFormat:TextFormat = new TextFormat();
myTextFormat.align = TextFormatAlign.LEFT;
myTextFormat.size = 12;

myTextField.defaultTextFormat = myTextFormat;
myTextField.autoSize = TextFieldAutoSize.LEFT;
myTextField.multiline = true;
myTextField.wordWrap = true;
myTextField.text = "Your text here, with multiple columns separated by spaces or new lines.";

addChild(myTextField);
```

This code creates a text field, sets its text format, and adds it to the stage. The text will be displayed in a single column by default. To create multiple columns, you can split your text into separate lines or use a more complex layout approach, such as using a `TextFlow` object for better control over text formatting and positioning.

For more advanced text layout, consider using the `TextFlow` class, which provides more flexibility for handling multiple columns and complex text formatting. Here is an example of how to use `TextFlow`:

```actionscript
import flash.text.TextFlow;
import flash.text.TextLine;
import flash.text.TextLineType;
import flash.text.TextLineFormat;
import flash.text.TextLineMetrics;

var myTextFlow:TextFlow = new TextFlow();
var myTextFormat:TextFormat = new TextFormat();
myTextFormat.size = 12;
myTextFormat.align = TextFormatAlign.LEFT;

var myTextLine:TextLine = new TextLine(myTextFormat, "Your text here, with multiple columns separated by spaces or new lines.");
myTextFlow.addChild(myTextLine);

var myTextLineFormat:TextLineFormat = new TextLineFormat();
myTextLineFormat.size = 12;
myTextLineFormat.align = TextFormatAlign.LEFT;

var myTextLineMetrics:TextLineMetrics = new TextLineMetrics();
myTextLineMetrics.maxWidth = 200; // Set the maximum width for the text line

myTextLineMetrics.setTextFormat(myTextLineFormat);
myTextLineMetrics.setText(myTextFlow);

addChild(myTextFlow);
```

This code sets up a `TextFlow` with a single `TextLine`, but you can add multiple `TextLine` objects to the `TextFlow` to create multiple columns. Adjust the `maxWidth` property to control the width of each column.

Remember to test and adjust the layout to fit your specific needs.

